https://www.saucedemo.com/ and Selenium + TestNG.  
   
10. First Test the Project in Eclipse  
Before using Jenkins, make sure the test works in Eclipse.  
Right-click:  
SauceDemoTest.java  
Select:  
Run As → TestNG Test You should get:  
Tests Run: 3  
Failures: 0  
Skipped: 0  
Do this first because Jenkins cannot successfully execute a project that already fails because of its own configuration.  
   
11. Create Jenkins Job  
Go to Jenkins Dashboard.  
Click: New 
Item  
Enter:  
Selenium_Automation  
Select:  
Freestyle project Click: OK  
   
12. Configure Jenkins Job  
You will see the configuration page.  We will configure it step by step.  
   
13. General Section  
In the Description box, enter: Selenium WebDriver automation using TestNG 
   
14. Source Code Management  
If your project is not stored in GitHub/GitLab, for this simple classroom practical, you can skip Source Code Management. Select:  
None  
Later, a real CI project can use Git/GitHub.  
   
15. Build Section  
Scroll down to: 
Build Steps Click:  
Add build step → Execute Windows batch command Enter a command that runs your test project.  
If your project uses Maven  
Use: mvn clean test  
This is the recommended CI approach if your Selenium project is a Maven project.  
   
16. If You Are NOT Using Maven  
Since you have previously worked with normal Eclipse Java projects, you may not have Maven.  
In that case, the simplest classroom approach is to create a Maven version of the Selenium project before connecting it to Jenkins.  
A typical Maven project looks like:  
SeleniumJenkins  
│  
├── pom.xml  
│  
└── src  
    └── test  
        └── java  
            └── tests  
                └── 
SauceDemoTest.java Then Jenkins can simply execute: mvn clean test  
This is much easier than manually maintaining a long Java classpath inside Jenkins.  
   
17. Add TestNG Report  
Scroll down to: Postbuild Actions Click:  
Add post-build action Select:  
Publish TestNG Results  
If you don't see this option, install the TestNG Results plugin from:  Manage Jenkins → Plugins Then return to your job configuration.  In the Results field enter:  
test-output/testng-results.xml or, for Maven/Surefire-based execution: target/surefire-reports/testngresults.xml  
Use the path that your project actually generates.  
   
