23. Build History  
Now execute the Jenkins job several times.  
For example:  
Build History  
  
#5   SUCCESS  
#4   SUCCESS  
#3   FAILURE  
#2   SUCCESS  
#1   SUCCESS  
This allows us to analyze previous test executions.  
   
24. Analyze Test Execution History  
Suppose the history shows: Build 
 Result  Tests  
#1  	SUCCESS  	3 
#2  	SUCCESS  	3 
#3  	FAILURE  	3 
#4  	SUCCESS  	3 
#5  	SUCCESS  	3 
We can conclude:  
●	Builds #1 and #2 passed. ●  	Build #3 failed.  
●	Builds #4 and #5 passed.  
●	The failure can be investigated using Console Output and Test Results.  
   
25. Automatic Execution  
To make Jenkins execute automatically, configure:  
Build Triggers  
For a simple demonstration, select: Build periodically Example:  
H/5 * * * *  
This means Jenkins schedules the job approximately every 5 minutes. For a classroom practical, you can also use: Poll SCM  when the project is connected to a source-control repository.  
For a real CI workflow, the preferred setup is generally:  
Developer changes code  
        ↓  
Git repository  
        ↓  
Jenkins trigger  
        ↓ Build  
        ↓  
Selenium tests  
        ↓ Report  
   
26. Important Point About Selenium and Jenkins  
When Jenkins runs Selenium tests, Jenkins must be able to access the browser and the required WebDriver environment.  
For a simple local setup:  
Jenkins  
   ↓  
Windows machine  
   ↓  
Firefox  
   ↓  
GeckoDriver  
   ↓  
Selenium Test  
